t I see the bucket here if I open  the bucket I see the original image  which is multiple megabytes in size  and the original that's that large image  click it aha here it is  all right and then uh here in the bucket  next to the original is the thumbnail  version of it  S size in bytes is much smaller than the  original image of course  if I click the thumbnail uh-huh I see  that its dimensions are four smaller  than the original so everything's  working uh Terry how did you design this  application  well I wanted to write an example  application that was event driven right  reacting to image uploads is probably  one of the easiest and most canonical uh  to understand examples right so once  this was created I needed a way to see  the image so I created the the second  app the web app actually if you look at  the architecture diagram the most  important part of the app is the cloud  function here it's triggered when a new  file is uploaded to cloud storage bucket  the function contains the image resizing  code  okay why not resize the image in the  cloud run service over here it handles  the upload after all what files can be  uploaded in many ways more than just  through this app maybe there's a mobile  app or maybe another microservice that  uploads files to the bucket or maybe  someone puts files directly in the  bucket maybe there's a batch process  somewhere uh somewhere else that someone  is uploading lots of images at once  instead of doing everything in the  uploader we can keep things decoupled by  using events in this case we run a  function whenever the file file added or  file created event is fired so the cloud  run service is only there to provide an  easy way to upload images it's not  really part of the event processing  that's right ah I see that cloudron does  two things it serves HTML to the browser  and it uploads files in our last video  you used nginx to serve HTML I left out  nginx for this app for to keep things  simple right like Cloud run 